# One compiler, one compile per shader.
#
# zshader links libslang once and is the ONLY shader compiler driver in this
# build. There is no slangc binary to find, probe, version-match or put on
# PATH, and no "replay" of a cook for reflection: the same linked program
# answers both "what does this module declare" and "what are its bytes".
#
# cmake writes ONE declarative manifest from the table below and runs zshader
# ONCE. That invocation compiles every entry point in-process (shared Slang
# sessions and parsed modules across worker threads), extracts reflection,
# writes the .spv files hot reload watches, and emits:
#
#   ShaderBindings.hpp        Slang's reflection of the same linked programs
#   ShaderBytecode.cpp        the SPIR-V, inlined as C++ arrays (no #embed of
#                             an individual .spv, no per-module file edge)
#   GeneratedGpuTypes.hpp     the gpu_abi module's reflected host layout
#   gpu_abi.spv               proof the ABI module codegens for compute
#
# Editing one shader re-runs the batch, but the batch IS the compile: there is
# no second process, no second parse, no second linker invocation. The old
# 400-line accumulation of --bytes / --slang-source / --slang-define flags,
# four global CMake lists and twenty per-target custom commands is gone.

# Outputs of the single zshader command. Ninja knows every one of them, so a
# shader edit rebuilds exactly the generated byte TU and relinks.
